DESCRIPTION:Title: How Do Ion Beams Probe and Reshape Materials?\n\nAbstrac
 t: \n\nIon beams provide a powerful way to both characterize materials and
  deliberately modify their structure. When energetic ions enter a solid\, 
 they interact with atomic nuclei and electrons. Analysis of the resulting 
 scattered particles and other signals can reveal crystalline structure\, l
 attice defects\, elemental composition\, and depth-dependent distributions
 . Rutherford backscattering spectrometry (RBS) combined with ion channelin
 g can quantify crystalline disorder and track how radiation damage accumul
 ates or recovers. At the same time\, energetic ions drive materials far fr
 om equilibrium. Energy transferred through collisions with atomic nuclei p
 roduces atomic displacements and collision cascades\, whereas energy trans
 ferred to electrons generates electronic excitation and ionization. These 
 processes can create defects and disorder\, but they can also promote defe
 ct recombination\, atomic rearrangement\, grain-boundary motion\, recrysta
 llization\, and phase transformation. Increasing evidence shows that elect
 ronic excitation can interact strongly with displacement damage\, producin
 g structural responses that cannot be understood from atomic collisions al
 one.\n\nBy controlling ion species\, energy\, fluence\, and irradiation te
 mperature\, ion beams can therefore be used to direct defect populations a
 nd microstructure. This presentation will introduce the fundamental physic
 s of ion-solid interactions and explain these concepts using materials wit
 h contrasting bonding and electronic structures\, including silicon carbid
 e\, complex oxides\, and metallic alloys. Examples will show how crystal s
 tructure\, bonding\, and chemical complexity influence the balance between
  defect production and recovery\, as well as interface and phase stability
 . Together\, ion beam analysis and modification provide complementary appr
 oaches for linking atomic-scale radiation processes to materials performan
 ce and for developing radiation-tolerant materials for advanced energy tec
 hnologies\, electronics\, and other extreme environments.
GEO:33.21307;-97.146589
LOCATION:Physics Building\, 104
